emoji.txt is Hex's own picks (MIT like the rest of Hex): each word was read alongside the emoji these sources link to
it, as scripts/emoji.py prints them, and given the ones someone typing it would want, or none:

Unicode's emoji-test.txt, Emoji 16.0, https://unicode.org/Public/emoji/16.0/, CLDR 46's English annotations,
https://github.com/unicode-org/cldr/blob/release-46/common/annotations/en.xml, and Unicode's emoji frequency,
https://home.unicode.org/emoji/emoji-frequency/ (all under the Unicode License v3, https://www.unicode.org/license.txt,
Copyright © 1991-2024 Unicode, Inc.): the fully-qualified emoji, each one's name and keywords, and how much it is used.

